# Tag

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A tag names a status in a word or two, such as “Available” or “Completed”. It describes something. It is not an action, and people cannot press it.

Home Assistant has no tag element. A tag is plain markup on this site. This page owns the Civica pattern and says what Home Assistant offers for the same job.

This page covers:

- Tag
  - Civica Tag Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A tag is a short, rectangular label that sits next to the thing it describes, in reading order. The words carry the meaning and the colour is a second cue. A tag is not a link or a button.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-tag-pattern"><code>Civica Tag Pattern</code></h3>

The pattern has a default tag and coloured variants, such as green, grey and blue. Use the smallest set of statuses that helps, and use the same word for the same state everywhere.

**State label or attention count.** Use the [shared state vocabulary and message decision](status.html#choose-the-right-message). This page owns the rectangular, non-interactive status label. Use the [Badge](notification-badge.html) only for a count of new or unread items, and a [banner](notification-banners.html) when a complete message needs scope and an action. Do not turn a tag into any of those by changing its colour or adding a click handler.

**What Home Assistant draws instead.** None of these is a tag:

- Badges and labels on cards, such as the badges on a Heading card (see [Heading](heading.html)).
- Chips, which are small and interactive (see [Chip](chip.html)).
- The state text on a Tile card or an Entities row.

**Markdown card.** It can show a status word in `strong` or `mark`, but not a coloured pill, because classes and styles are removed (see [Markdown Card](markdown-card.html)). Write the state as a plain word in sentence case.

**Theme colours.** The theme defines Civica’s own status colour pairs for success, danger, warning and notice. They are Civica variables that Home Assistant does not read, so they do not colour Home Assistant’s own badges. They work only where Civica’s own code, or card-mod, refers to them. Home Assistant’s own pills take their colours from its colour tokens.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A tag turns a state into one scannable word.
- A coloured tag is not a Home Assistant element, so a dashboard cannot draw it in a Markdown card.
- A tag can say too little. It does not say why a device is unavailable or what to do.
- Too many tags make a page noisy. Avoid giving every normal reading a prominent label.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a short label when it helps people understand a device, task or record. Start with the smallest useful set of statuses and keep the vocabulary consistent. Use sentence case and visible words. Do not put a link inside a tag or start it with a verb such as “Start”, because that suggests an action. Place any associated action next to the tag, as its own control.

Say the state as it is:

- “Unavailable” is not “Off”. A lost connection does not prove the physical state.
- A setup task can be “Completed” while its device is unavailable. These are two facts and need two labels.
- A missing value must not become a success state.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.4.1 Use of Color: the word says the status, so colour is never the only cue.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum): tag text needs 4.5:1 against its fill. Test each theme in light and dark modes.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the text states the status in the page, not only in a colour or an image.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text, 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: tags wrap and stay readable at 200% text and 320 CSS pixels.
- 3.1.2 Language of Parts: translated labels carry their language.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: a tag that changes to report a result needs an announcement. A tag that is always there is not a status message.

The same word for the same state, kept short and free of jargon, keeps the effort low. Test without colour, with longer translations and at 200% text, using the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html). Review every state change so a missing value never reads as success.

